<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav current_user={@current_user} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="deployment"
      updated="August 4, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"fleet-shape", "The shape of a production fleet"},
        {"phased-rollout", "Roll out in phases"},
        {"worked-examples", "Two worked examples"},
        {"monitoring", "What to watch once it's live"},
        {"go-live-checklist", "Go-live checklist"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="deployment">
        <:dek>
          The
          <.link navigate={~p"/docs/quickstart"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
            quickstart
          </.link>
          put one runner on one host. Production is a different question: dozens of hosts, real
          blast radius, an agent you won't hand a shell — and a security review that will ask
          exactly how a wrong call gets stopped.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        None of it is much configuration. The defaults are already conservative — reads run,
        mutations wait for a human, <code class="font-mono">critical</code>
        is denied outright. Going to production is mostly deciding <em>where</em>
        to tighten, and doing it once.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="fleet-shape">The shape of a production fleet</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Two decisions carry most of the safety, and you make both up front: how runners are <strong class="text-zinc-200">grouped</strong>, and which people are
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">scoped</strong>
        to reach them — an agent's key is bounded by its owner's scope, so scoping the person
        scopes their agent. Get these right and the rest is defaults.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        (How to install runners and where each shape can act — a <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/host-install"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >host</.link>, a <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/containers"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >container</.link>,
        a
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/kubernetes"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Kubernetes</.link>
        or
        <.link navigate={~p"/docs/nomad"} class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">Nomad</.link>
        node — is its own set of pages.)
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Group by tier, not by host.</strong>
            A
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/runners" <> "#groups-labels"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >group</.link>
            is the unit that policy, runbooks, member scopes, and agent fan-out all target.
            Name it for the fleet role — <code class="font-mono">web</code>, <code class="font-mono">edge</code>, <code class="font-mono">db-prod</code>,
            <code class="font-mono">cache</code>
            — not the machine. The installer defaults the group
            to the hostname; change it on your second host. <code class="font-mono">web-01</code>
            is a label, not a group.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Labels for the cross-cutting facts.</strong>
            Free-form <code class="font-mono">key=value</code>
            pairs — <code class="font-mono">region=us-east-1</code>, <code class="font-mono">environment=prod</code>,
            <code class="font-mono">service=checkout</code>
            — for filtering and searching the fleet without redrawing your groups. (Policy targets a
            runner, a group, or the account — not labels.)
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Keep policy and access aligned.</strong>
            Policy decides what a runner will <em>run</em>; access decides who can <em>reach</em>
            it. A locked-down database tier should have both — a strict ruleset <em>and</em>
            only the DBAs (and their agent) in scope.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p class="mt-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Here's an example of how you might shape your runner fleet:
      </p>
      <div class="mt-3 divide-y divide-zinc-900 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[8rem_1fr]">
          <div class="font-mono text-sm text-brand-300">web</div>
          <div class="text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            10 hosts · packs <code class="font-mono">nginx</code>, <code class="font-mono">linux-core</code>, <code class="font-mono">debugging</code>.
            Reads run on policy; a restart waits for one approval. The everyday tier.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[8rem_1fr]">
          <div class="font-mono text-sm text-amber-300">db-prod</div>
          <div class="text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            3 hosts · packs <code class="font-mono">postgres</code>, <code class="font-mono">linux-core</code>. Locked: a targeted ruleset denies <code class="font-mono">critical</code>, four-eyes on <code class="font-mono">high</code>. Only the DBA team in scope.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-1 p-4 sm:grid-cols-[8rem_1fr]">
          <div class="font-mono text-sm text-zinc-300">staging</div>
          <div class="text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
            2 hosts · the full catalog, including the <code class="font-mono">shell</code>
            break-glass pack. Looser so an agent can iterate on a fix — never a path to a prod host.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="phased-rollout">Roll out in phases</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each phase is safe to stop at, and each is reversible. You never have a half-configured
        fleet that's more open than the phase before it.
      </p>
      <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-6 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pilot on one non-prod host.</strong>
          Install, watch it connect, run a read, then open the row from <a
            href={~p"/app/audit"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >Audit</a>. Prove the loop end to
          end before it touches anything that matters.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Configure the policy.</strong>
          Decide what each risk tier may do across the whole fleet. A good policy is strict where the
          blast radius is real and relaxed only where you've decided it's safe — and the editor won't
          let a higher tier be more permissive than a lower one. See <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/policies-and-approvals"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >policies &amp; approvals</.link>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Add approvals where they matter.</strong>
          For anything that can drop a service or lose data, turn on four-eyes — two distinct
          approvers, self-approval off. Approvers get an email with the request.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Scope people and agents.</strong>
          Give each member only the runners or groups they own — their agents' keys are bounded
          by that same scope. Out-of-scope hosts are not visible to the operators.
          See <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/teams-and-access" <> "#runner-scopes"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >per-member runner scopes</.link>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Lock the sensitive groups.</strong>
          Add a targeted ruleset for your production tiers. A ruleset <em>replaces</em>
          the default for its target (most specific wins: runner over group over default), so the
          <code class="font-mono">db-prod</code>
          group can deny <code class="font-mono">critical</code>
          and four-eyes <code class="font-mono">high</code>
          while the rest of the fleet stays on the default.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Wire identity.</strong>
          Require MFA account-wide. Add
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/sso"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >SSO</.link> <.plan_note tier={:team} /> so people sign in through your IdP; then add
          <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/scim"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >SCIM</.link> <.plan_note tier={:enterprise} />
          so offboarding someone in the directory suspends them here —
          sessions killed, keys revoked — with no checklist.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-zinc-200">Ingest the audit into your SIEM.</strong>
          Mint an
          <a
            href={~p"/app/audit/export"}
            class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
          >audit-export token</a>
          and point a collector at the NDJSON export API — emisar doesn't push, so something
          on your side polls it — then alert on the handful of events that actually mean something
          (below) — see <.link
            navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem"}
            class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
          >audit &amp; SIEM</.link>. <.plan_note tier={:team} />
        </li>
      </ol>

      <.docs_h2 id="worked-examples">Two worked examples</.docs_h2>

      <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-950/60 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">
          An AI agent that reads the web tier and restarts behind approval
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <strong class="text-zinc-300">Goal:</strong>
          your incident agent should investigate nginx freely, and be able to restart it — but a
          restart should need a human nod.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-zinc-300">Fleet:</strong>
              the ten web hosts share group <code class="font-mono">web</code>, packs
              <code class="font-mono">nginx</code>
              + <code class="font-mono">linux-core</code>
              + <code class="font-mono">debugging</code>.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-zinc-300">Policy:</strong>
              the defaults already fit — log tails and status reads are <code class="font-mono">low</code>/<code class="font-mono">medium</code>
              (allow); <code class="font-mono">linux.systemctl_restart</code>
              is <code class="font-mono">high</code>
              (require approval). No override to write.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-zinc-300">Access:</strong>
              the operator is scoped to the <code class="font-mono">web</code>
              group — they can't reach any other tier — and their agent's key inherits that
              scope.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <strong class="text-zinc-300">Result:</strong>
          the agent calls <code class="font-mono">nginx.error_tail</code>
          and it runs; it calls <code class="font-mono">linux.systemctl_restart</code>
          and the run holds for the on-call, who sees the host, the exact arguments, and the agent's
          reason before approving. Every call lands in the audit with its client, key, and reason.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-zinc-800 bg-zinc-950/60 p-5">
        <h3 class="text-base font-semibold text-zinc-100">
          A destructive action on db-prod, done safely
        </h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <strong class="text-zinc-300">Goal:</strong>
          killing a stuck Postgres backend on <code class="font-mono">db-prod</code>
          should be possible — but only when two people approve.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-zinc-300">Fleet:</strong>
              group <code class="font-mono">db-prod</code>, three hosts, packs
              <code class="font-mono">postgres</code>
              + <code class="font-mono">linux-core</code>.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
            <span>
              <strong class="text-zinc-300">Policy + approvals:</strong>
              a targeted ruleset for the group — <code class="font-mono">critical</code>
              denied, <code class="font-mono">high</code>
              require approval — with four-eyes on: two approvers, self-approval off.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
          <strong class="text-zinc-300">The flow:</strong>
          a DBA's agent calls <code class="font-mono">postgres.terminate_backend</code>
          with a reason. Policy holds the run for approval; two other operators approve —
          self-approval is off, so the person who asked can't be one of them. The twice-approved run
          dispatches and the runner executes it. The audit shows the requester, both approvers, and
          the redacted command. Nobody drops a connection on
          <code class="font-mono">db-prod</code>
          alone, and every step is on the record.
        </p>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="monitoring">What to watch once it's live</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Five signals cover a governed fleet. Each one has a place it's visible today and a
        consequence if you ignore it — that consequence is the reason it's on the list, not the
        metric itself.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Runners online, and on which version.</strong>
            The Runners page shows both. An offline runner cannot accept new dispatches; a queued
            run ends as an error after the grace period if the host stays away, while an action
            already executing may continue and settle after reconnect. Watch the version alongside
            it: unsupported peers are warned about today and can be refused when compatibility
            enforcement is enabled — see <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/upgrades" <> "#compatibility"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >compatibility status</.link>.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Failed, errored, and denied runs.</strong>
            Failures are the fleet talking; denials are the policy talking. A rising denial rate
            from one actor usually means a scope or ruleset no longer matches how people work —
            fix the rule rather than the symptom.
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/runs"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Runs &amp; history</.link>
            owns the states.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">How long approvals wait.</strong>
            A request nobody answers is an outage with a polite name: the run never dispatches and
            the person who asked eventually works around you. Track the age of the oldest pending
            request against whatever your on-call promises.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Pending trust and drifted packs.</strong>
            New bytes on a runner are pending until someone trusts the exact hash, and a hash that
            no longer matches blocks dispatch by design. Both are visible on the Packs page, and
            both mean actions are unavailable until a human looks.
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/pack-updates"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Roll out and roll back packs</.link>
            owns the procedure.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">Your audit collector's cursor.</strong>
            The export is a pull API, so a stalled poller is silent — the console keeps recording
            while your SIEM sees nothing. Alert on the cursor not advancing and on repeated failed
            polls, and fix it before the oldest un-ingested event ages out of retention.
            <.link
              navigate={~p"/docs/audit-and-siem" <> "#health"}
              class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
            >Audit &amp; SIEM</.link>
            owns poller health.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Prove all five before you widen anything: run one bounded canary — a single low-risk,
        read-only action on one non-critical runner — and confirm it succeeds, appears in the run
        history, and lands in your SIEM through the collector rather than through the console. That
        last step is the one people skip, and it's the only one that tests the whole path. Repeat it
        after every runner upgrade and every pack rollout.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="go-live-checklist">Go-live checklist</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        The list to walk before you point production at emisar — the same one a security reviewer
        will:
      </p>
      <.docs_callout kind={:note} icon="hero-shield-check">
        <ul class="space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-300">
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>
              Every runner runs as the non-root <code class="font-mono">emisar</code>
              user — the installer default; confirm nobody changed it.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>
              Runners are grouped by tier; every production group carries its own locked ruleset.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>
              <code class="font-mono">critical</code>
              is denied fleet-wide; <code class="font-mono">high</code>
              requires approval.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>Four-eyes is on for any group that can lose data or drop a service.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>
              Members are scoped to the hosts they own — agent keys inherit that scope, so
              nothing is account-wide by accident.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>MFA is required; SSO is wired on Team or Enterprise, SCIM on Enterprise.</span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>
              Pack secrets live in <code class="font-mono">runner.env</code>
              + <code class="font-mono">inherit_env</code>, never in call arguments.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>
              The <code class="font-mono">shell</code>
              break-glass pack is on no production runner.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>
              Signed dispatch is enforced wherever bridge attribution must be provable. A
              customer-authorized bridge signs each request with a key the cloud never holds.
            </span>
          </li>
          <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
            <.icon name="hero-check" class="mt-1 h-4 w-4 flex-none text-brand-400" />
            <span>
              A collector is polling the audit export API with an
              <code class="font-mono">audit-export</code>
              token, its cursor is advancing, and alerts are wired on policy changes and admission
              blocks.
            </span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </.docs_callout>

      <.docs_callout kind={:tip} title="On Enterprise, you don't do this alone.">
        A security and procurement review, a rollout plan mapped to your fleet, and design-partner
        support come with the plan. Tell us what you're running and we'll help you land it —
        <.link href={~p"/pricing"} class="font-medium text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300">
          see plans
        </.link>
        or email <a
          href={EmisarWeb.MailTo.sales()}
          class="font-medium text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >sales@emisar.dev</a>.
      </.docs_callout>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
